---
import VisaPage from '../../../components/ui/VisaPage.astro';
---
<VisaPage
  lang="en"
  title="Panama Friendly Nations Visa"
  description="Complete guide to Panama's Friendly Nations visa: all 50 eligible countries, three qualifying routes, 2-year provisional to permanent residency and path to citizenship."
  visaName="Panama Friendly Nations Visa"
  tag="50 eligible countries"
  tagColor="bg-gold/20 text-slate"
  mainReq="Citizen of eligible country + one qualifying economic link"
  processingTime="2–3 months (provisional) · up to 24 months to permanent"
  renewalNote="2-year provisional residency → permanent residency → citizenship after 5 years"
  intro="One of Panama's most flexible residency routes, available to nationals of 50 countries that maintain professional, economic, and friendship ties with Panama. The Friendly Nations visa requires establishing just one qualifying economic link — not a guaranteed pension, not a fixed amount of capital — which makes it accessible to a wide range of profiles: remote workers, entrepreneurs, professionals, and retirees alike. After the 2-year provisional period, you can apply for permanent residency, and after 5 years of permanent residency, you become eligible for Panamanian citizenship."
  requirements={[
    "Be a citizen of one of the 50 eligible countries (full list below in the FAQ)",
    "Establish one qualifying economic link — choose any one of these three routes:",
    "Route 1 — Real estate: purchase real estate in Panama with a registered value of at least $200,000 USD, held personally or through a corporate structure where you are the ultimate beneficial owner",
    "Route 2 — Fixed-term bank deposit: place a deposit of at least $200,000 USD for a minimum of 3 years in any bank holding a General License regulated by the Superintendency of Banks of Panama",
    "Route 3 — Panama employment: hold a formal employment contract with a Panama-registered company, subject to obtaining the corresponding work permit (permiso de trabajo)",
    "If you hold dual nationality and one passport is from an eligible country, you may use that passport for the entire application — even if your other nationality is not on the eligible list",
    "Valid passport with at least 6 months of validity remaining, plus complete authenticated copies",
    "National criminal background check from your country of origin — apostilled, maximum 6 months old",
    "Apostilled civil documents: birth certificate; marriage certificate if including a spouse",
    "Evidence of your chosen qualifying route (property deed, bank certificate, or signed employment contract with work permit)",
    "Medical exam performed in Panama",
    "Health insurance with active coverage valid in Panama",
    "Passport-size photos and payment of government fees",
    "All foreign documents must be apostilled or authenticated by the Panama Consulate and translated by an Authorized Public Translator in Panama"
  ]}
  benefits={[
    "2-year provisional residency → permanent residency after the provisional period is completed",
    "The only visa that includes an integrated work permit: formal employment in Panama is legal from day one",
    "Freedom to open bank accounts, register companies, and sign contracts in Panama",
    "Eligible for Panamanian citizenship after 5 years of permanent residency",
    "Spouse and minor children can be included as dependents on the same application",
    "Parents of the main applicant (no age limit) and dependent students aged 18–25 may also qualify",
    "Compatible with remote work for foreign companies — Panama does not tax foreign-source income",
    "Dual nationals can use the eligible country passport for the entire process"
  ]}
  notFor={[
    "Your country is not among the 50 eligible nations — check the Qualified Investor, Rentista Retirado, or Pensionado visas instead",
    "You cannot establish any of the three qualifying links (no $200K for real estate or fixed deposit, and no Panama employment contract)",
    "You want permanent residency from day one — the Friendly Nations visa begins with a 2-year provisional period",
    "You need immediate unrestricted permanent residency — consider the Qualified Investor (Golden Visa) if you have the capital"
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "Which 50 countries are eligible for the Panama Friendly Nations Visa?",
      a: "The 50 eligible countries are: Germany, Andorra, Argentina, Australia, Austria, Belgium, Brazil, Canada, Chile, Cyprus, South Korea, Costa Rica, Croatia, Denmark, Spain, United States, Estonia, Finland, France, Greece, Hong Kong, Hungary, Ireland, Israel, Italy, Japan, Latvia, Liechtenstein, Lithuania, Luxembourg, Malta, San Marino, Mexico, Monaco, Montenegro, Norway, New Zealand, Netherlands, Paraguay, Poland, Portugal, United Kingdom, Czech Republic, Singapore, South Africa, Sweden, Switzerland, Taiwan, Uruguay. If you hold dual nationality and one of your passports is from any country on this list, you can use that passport for the entire application."
    },
    {
      q: "What are the three qualifying economic links for the Friendly Nations visa?",
      a: "You must establish exactly one qualifying link — you choose which one fits your situation: (1) Real estate: purchase Panamanian property worth at least $200,000 USD. Multiple properties may be combined; the investment can be held personally or through a corporate structure. (2) Fixed-term bank deposit: deposit $200,000 USD for a minimum of 3 years in any bank with a General License in Panama. (3) Panama employment: obtain a formal employment contract with a Panama-registered company and the corresponding work permit. You only need one of these three; you do not need to combine them."
    },
    {
      q: "How long does the Friendly Nations visa process take?",
      a: "The provisional 2-year residency card is typically issued within 2 to 3 months of filing a complete application. The process then continues for up to 24 months, during which you live in Panama maintaining your qualifying link (keeping the property, deposit, or employment). After the provisional period ends, you apply to convert to permanent residency. After 5 years of permanent residency, Panamanian citizenship becomes available."
    },
    {
      q: "Can I work in Panama with the Friendly Nations visa?",
      a: "Yes — and this is one of the defining advantages of this visa. The Friendly Nations visa with the employment route includes a formal work permit (permiso de trabajo), allowing you to be employed by a Panama company. Those who qualify via the real estate or fixed deposit routes can generally operate their own businesses in Panama; formal salaried employment under someone else would require a separate work permit. For remote workers earning income from outside Panama, the visa is also compatible — Panama does not tax foreign-source income."
    },
    {
      q: "What happens after the 2-year provisional period?",
      a: "After completing the 2-year provisional residency period — while maintaining your qualifying economic link — you apply to convert your status to permanent residency. Panama's immigration service reviews the application and, if approved, issues permanent resident status with no expiration. From the date permanent residency is granted, you begin the 5-year countdown toward eligibility for Panamanian citizenship and a Panamanian passport."
    }
  ]}
/>
